  "_exec_summary": [
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000124294,
      "SummaryText": "EXECUTIVE SUMMARY:\n\nRisk Management Plan executive summary to comply with 40 CRF \u00a7 68.155 as amended by [61 FR 31726, June 20, 1996, as amended at 69 FR 18831, Apr. 9, 2004].\n\nLocation: Air Liquide Electronics US LP, 19 Steel Road West, Morrisville, PA 19067\n\nTHE ACCIDENTAL RELEASE PREVENTION AND EMERGENCY RESPONSE POLICIES AT THE STATIONARY SOURCE:\nAir Liquide Electronics U.S. LP is committed to: endorsing the principles of Safety First and Zero Accidents, operating safely and reliably in compliance with all applicable laws, performing in an environmentally responsible manner, consistently producing products and services that meet or exceed our customer's expectations, driving continual improvement and innovation & effectively applying lessons learned.\n\nAir Liquide is a member of the American Chemistry Council (ACC) with a commitment to meet all the code requirements of the Responsible Care Program.\n\nThe Morrisville plant complies with Air Liquide Electronics U.S. LP (ALEUS) policies on process design to reduce probability of release or accident, risk assessment for new and existing processes, incident investigation and reporting, management of change, ready for startup review, preventive maintenance, and training as accidental release prevention.\n\nThe Morrisville plant maintains, follows, and trains personnel on an Emergency Action Plan.\n\nSince PHA sections are typically reviewed for possible improvements before a \"five year update\" is required, it is common to have additional improvements (action items) that are implemented as part of continuous improvement (ex. considerations for facility siting are considered when other changes are proposed for the site). \n\nEXECUTIVE SUMMARY:\nRisk Management Plan executive summary to comply with 40 CFR \u00a7 68.155 as amended by [61 FR 31726, June 20, 1996, as amended at 69 FR 18831, Apr. 9, 2004].\n\nLocation: Air Liquide Electronics US LP, 19 Steel Road West, Morrisville, PA 19067\n\nTHE ACCIDENTAL RELEASE PREVENTION AND EMERGENCY RE"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000124294,
      "SummaryText": "SPONSE POLICIES AT THE\nSTATIONARY SOURCE:\nAir Liquide Electronics U.S. LP is committed to: endorsing the principles of Safety First and Zero Accidents, operating safely and reliably in compliance with all applicable laws, performing in an environmentally responsible manner, consistently producing products and services that meet or exceed our customer's expectations, driving continual improvement and innovation & effectively applying lessons learned.\nThe Morrisville plant complies with Air Liquide Electronics U.S. LP (ALEUS) policies on process\ndesign to reduce probability of release or accident, risk assessment for new and existing processes, incident investigation and reporting, management of change, ready for startup review, preventive maintenance, and training as accidental release prevention.\nThe Morrisville plant maintains, follows, and trains personnel on an Emergency Action Plan.\nSince PHA sections are typically reviewed for possible improvements before a \"five year update\" is required, it is common to have additional improvements (action items) that are implemented as part of continuous improvement (ex. considerations for facility siting are considered when other changes are proposed for the site).\n\nTHE STATIONARY SOURCE AND REGULATED SUBSTANCES HANDLED:\nThe Air Liquide Morrisville, PA plant primary activity is transfer of product from a bulk source into compressed gas cylinders. This type of activity is commonly referred to as repackaging. The covered processes at the Morrisville Plant are chlorine re-packaging, chlorine storage, silane re-packaging, silane storage, boron trichloride storage, and\ndichlorosilane storage.\n\nTHE GENERAL ACCIDENTAL RELEASE PREVENTION PROGRAM AND CHEMICAL-SPECIFIC\nPREVENTION STEPS:\nThe chemical specific accidental release and accident prevention steps are those to mitigate risk from toxic and flammable gases. This facility complies with EPAs accident prevention rule, the OSHA PSM standard, and all applicable state and local codes"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 2,
      "FacilityID": 1000124294,
      "SummaryText": "and regulations. Air Liquide Morrisville location utilizes the following accidental release prevention and emergency response policies at the stationary source:\na) Management of change to ensure the covered process is not altered without a safety review\nb) Operation following written operating procedures\nc) Training in the process for all operators\nd) Preventative maintenance\ne) Pre-startup safety review\nf) Near miss and incident investigation with corrective and preventative actions\ng) Hazard review by process hazard analysis procedure\nh) Use of pressure vessels covered under DOT\ni) Area electrical classification and conforming equipment\nj) Hazard communication (OSHA Hazcomm)\nk) Safety program with monthly meetings\nl) Hot work program\nm) Safe work program\nAir Liquide Morrisville location utilizes the following chemical specific prevention steps at the\nstationary source:\na) Leak checking equipment prior to operation per standard operating procedure\nb) Scrubbers to mitigate chemical discharge\nc) Pressure relief devices as specified by DOT and/or ASME\nd) Hazardous gas monitoring and supervised alarms\ne) Process hazard identification by process hazard analysis\nf) Check valves\ng) Fire detectors, sprinklers and alarms\nh) Operating by following written operating procedures\ni) Emergency action plan training\nj) Operating and maintenance training\nk) Management of change for all changes\nl) Lockout followed (safe work program)\nm) Burst disc\nn) Ventilation\no) Preventative maintenance\np) Pressure gauges\nq) Housekeeping\nr) Personal protective equipment\n\nTHE FIVE-YEAR ACCIDENT HISTORY:\nAir Liquide Morrisville location has never had an accident involving chlorine, silane, boron trichloride or dichlorosilane that caused deaths, injuries, property or environmental\ndamage, evacuations, or shelterings in place.\n\nTHE EMERGENCY RESPONSE PROGRAM:\nThe Morrisville location maintains its own team of emergency responders to handle emergencies involving chemical release. At the plant, a stand"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 3,
      "FacilityID": 1000124294,
      "SummaryText": "alone building has been designated as a command post center where ER equipment is stored and emergencies can be managed. The building also contains the key contact information to communicate to outside agencies and local authorities. The Emergency Response Team members have completed their initial 40 hour training requirement and also receive their annual required 8 hour refresher training. Plant communicates with the Bucks County Emergency Management, the Bucks County LEPC, the Morrisville Fire Department, and the  Falls Township Fire Department. The Morrisville Plant maintains a facility Emergency Action Plan that has been shared with the Local Emergency Planning Committee (LEPC). The plan details the essential actions to take should an emergency occur. Plant personnel conduct periodic drills.\n\nPLANNED CHANGES TO IMPROVE SAFETY:\nAs is our policy at Air Liquide, we continue to review our process(es) for possible improvements to the process and to the operational methods and to evaluate new technology to lower risk. Air Liquide Morrisville, PA, conducts and resolves all findings from its PHA and other hazard analysis and utilizes a Management of Change Program to evaluate proposed changes prior to implementation.\nFor example, Morrisville plans on\n1) Identifying and labeling all Elements Important to Safety (EIS) components.\n2) Reviewing location of existing gas monitoring points and evaluating if more are needed."
    }
  ]
